%N A399416 Order array, U, of the array W given by w(n, k) = k*(n + Pi), for n >= 1, k >= 1, a rectangular array, read by descending antidiagonals; see Comments.
%C A399416 Suppose that (w(n, k)), for n >= 1 and k >= 1 is an array of distinct real numbers. Replace each w(n, k) by its position when all the numbers w(n, k) are ordered by <. The resulting array, U = (u(n, k)), is the order array of (w(n, k)). Every positive integer occurs exactly once in U, so that as a sequence, U is a permutation of the positive integers. Successive row terms, and column terms, have limiting ratio 1. See A399406 for a guide to related sequences.
%H A399416 <a href="/index/Per#IntegerPermutation">Index entries for sequences that are permutations of the natural numbers</a>
%F A399416 u(n,k) = Sum_{h = 1..floor(k*(n+x)/(1+x))} floor(k*(n+x)/h - x), where x = Pi.
%F A399416 u(n,k) = Sum_{m = 1..floor(k*(n+x) -x )} floor(k*(n+x)/(m+x)), where x = Pi.
%e A399416 Corner:
%e A399416    1    6   13   21   30   40   50   61   75
